{
  "description": "The lexical set the boundary code is decided from is the ROOT scope's own names, not every name written at the top of the file. `:b` is born inside a `repeat` body — a block scope — and a block encloses no procedure body, because a `define` is a phase-1 declaration rather than something nested in the scope it appears in (spec/execution-model.md:651-655). So the boundary is not what hid `:b` from `peek`, and the read is an ordinary `ol-undefined-var` (spec/error-model.md:102), not `ol-var-not-visible`. Added at @testing's request during the review gate, which measured that widening the scan to collect block-bound names too passed all 984 fixtures — the exact design decision under review had no stack-neutral test. This fixture is also what keeps issue #825's checker from diverging from the runtime on the same program.",
